[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-48238"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3027743251b98968805564e296d7570d80d35ff64b27cf831e631ed9c8efb40a.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-3,0)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-3$ and left-continuous at $x=0.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-3,0].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-3,0]$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-3,0]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[0,1]$ and $[0,4]$ contain the endpoint $x=0,$ but the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\n- $[-1,1]$ and $[-2,2]$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=0,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-48279/selection/cfde74fc8b3776833ae111ffe6c161cb9ccd114c990c2ac114fcab31266ee003" :answer/id #uuid "45008099-0a9a-5bef-82a2-0f66e7d2dce8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c5fedec0f96ef81c33146504cdabc8f0400ba79f77591167a285d119d238fd2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48279/selection/7605adeb7581757b2047fc6dd570cf2d8414f6eab6e1cb1e0abba06fffd990ae" :answer/id #uuid "6bb18bc0-9a99-5855-b7c6-0f74a53c2b27" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/387ed50e8983c83f96ea03bdd7a47d13d33a0c6e0728b52512fef8296351f73e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48279/selection/05a4a780f4b553b7c4eca24986ceab25dfab708adf688e26ce50d0c31cf15366" :answer/id #uuid "96ea7e20-13e0-5802-a213-2cdefded01e4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48279/selection/b60b6d39caad959e2cef8644695984144a3b7f5697266fe45ab8e75abd7c206d" :answer/id #uuid "71a8cbbb-31f6-54c3-8cc7-59c26a538c4c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/895c64a995d7f07a161a43945c369395b5cb365c5e7ec0f664df130c49795af8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48279/selection/c0dbd50b9d7c67d8ee010fa0c1b00f3009ac72b6541123ae2966e9efc77aff7e" :answer/id #uuid "4bd931eb-aff4-51b2-bf97-e00df57b9670" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/7f2b4da10350a6ba230e0ec1b0b26822b133ee4a9c1228a61565ed559efcdd64.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48279/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4de47f3-3670-52cc-9337-ef541d36c72a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48279/selection/05a4a780f4b553b7c4eca24986ceab25dfab708adf688e26ce50d0c31cf15366" :answer-field/choices ["q-48279/selection/cfde74fc8b3776833ae111ffe6c161cb9ccd114c990c2ac114fcab31266ee003" "q-48279/selection/7605adeb7581757b2047fc6dd570cf2d8414f6eab6e1cb1e0abba06fffd990ae" "q-48279/selection/05a4a780f4b553b7c4eca24986ceab25dfab708adf688e26ce50d0c31cf15366" "q-48279/selection/b60b6d39caad959e2cef8644695984144a3b7f5697266fe45ab8e75abd7c206d" "q-48279/selection/c0dbd50b9d7c67d8ee010fa0c1b00f3009ac72b6541123ae2966e9efc77aff7e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48279" :question/id #uuid "0790ffcc-e651-53c9-a00a-a9cbd12d21cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48279" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(1,3].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$, $h(x)$,  and $q(x)$ are not right-continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not continuous at  $x=2$, and so is not continuous on $(1,3].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-48279/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48279"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-48344"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/09d006953b4fc1d111a84e78fcc6b2038d53768fb82d6f61a0b2a1d0126bbb40.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in [-3,2]$\n- $x\\in (0,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-3,2].$ It is continuous on $(-3,2),$ and also right continuous at $x=-3$ and left continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(0,\\infty )$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-76091/selection/8d09ee9adcf71cb58d76b26ed7694fdd60e59618d05b86a8c43984b17a194f30" :answer/id #uuid "0716dac2-625d-56c5-8c47-9d76752261b9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c56ea94b0d5102c276d7cd4a196517a41611e3f7da82b3ab13c6b404c31cf430.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76091/selection/81e46dd50599eaffb88b6f37e9b8b7a20f42fcf8e3446e82516078ae607b2fb8" :answer/id #uuid "6b2ea36d-5419-51be-8d6d-b59e1d3029df" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76091/selection/84c12699a833fb5f417a8ad3264a5726b5b5baef8c262732e7dc5918d10e433e" :answer/id #uuid "cc44ad77-478a-5e1b-87ae-4441934bc6f9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/59bc174fabd3f93321505a54f05832fda46914c8f8f8ad5e88690f53bef3e845.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76091/selection/97732024f2dc9d8f252a5d55e1266b6f39246238c497195e4c245007cdf1e953" :answer/id #uuid "156b1b5f-b46f-5968-8e56-f0a0670df8b3" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/47dca98b1ac1c7d3310418c3d5eaf58416827aaa5d97d118aa5f3b4a6d59d9f8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76091/selection/665ae82790871f553674a8799eab00eb88040e71c419b671082575a75b7abb12" :answer/id #uuid "6a7caed5-7e4a-5703-af86-fc3c066955ef" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/27cc3b76baa60c48f103f7d016bb78716330dbb03be791458489e5458bf326bd.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76091/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2565675-69c4-5459-9308-0049091ec89f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76091/selection/81e46dd50599eaffb88b6f37e9b8b7a20f42fcf8e3446e82516078ae607b2fb8" :answer-field/choices ["q-76091/selection/8d09ee9adcf71cb58d76b26ed7694fdd60e59618d05b86a8c43984b17a194f30" "q-76091/selection/81e46dd50599eaffb88b6f37e9b8b7a20f42fcf8e3446e82516078ae607b2fb8" "q-76091/selection/84c12699a833fb5f417a8ad3264a5726b5b5baef8c262732e7dc5918d10e433e" "q-76091/selection/97732024f2dc9d8f252a5d55e1266b6f39246238c497195e4c245007cdf1e953" "q-76091/selection/665ae82790871f553674a8799eab00eb88040e71c419b671082575a75b7abb12"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76091" :question/id #uuid "b8055c33-18c1-54b4-a55d-b97e3d2f96f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76091" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-2,\\infty )?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png)\n\n    \n\nIndeed, we have that $h(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-2,\\infty ).$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$\n\n- $q(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not continuous at $x=1,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$\n\n- $g(x)$is not continuous at $x=2,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-76091/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76091"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76210/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3252e2e0-1955-5227-ad8f-abd15b84a594" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76210/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "d73ccb5f-3a85-59e2-9885-dcbf6e34c870" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76210/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "13d7c789-e62f-53d2-ab93-14153ebbe1f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76210/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c54bdda8-c62c-5bda-b721-accc2417173c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76210/selection/2ca4bfe4352389d647a1104a74259abfbc0ec6c68f7a958d38fd0bedad40d918" :answer/id #uuid "f4d38868-6ee5-58f5-aca1-4b5445eafad0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76210/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cef2f2a6-04a0-5767-81fd-e969532c0d69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76210/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-76210/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-76210/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-76210/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-76210/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-76210/selection/2ca4bfe4352389d647a1104a74259abfbc0ec6c68f7a958d38fd0bedad40d918"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76210" :question/id #uuid "70359685-3e4e-53a5-8fbd-53c281152289" :question/math-academy-id "q-76210" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/5e787dda0cccdafaa6a93176daea5f954b1cd1da0beaff410a6563536459208e.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$\n- $x\\in [-2,0]$\n- $x\\in [-1,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and also left continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-2,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-1,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(-1,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-76210/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76210"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
